---
name: "Create Spec Files"
description: "Writes behavior specs for the current change"
tags: [plan, openspec]
stage: plan
---
Read the current Mohist issue details before interpreting this task:

```bash
mo issue show ${{ issue.number }} --project ${{ issue.projectId }}
```

<artifact id="specs" schema="mohist-default">

<task>
Create specification files that define WHAT the system should do for this change.
Each spec describes the required behavior this change must deliver, as normative requirements and scenarios.
</task>

<dependencies>
Read these files for context before creating this artifact:

<dependency id="proposal" status="expected">
  <path>openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/proposal.md</path>
  <description>Initial proposal document outlining the change</description>
</dependency>
</dependencies>

<output>
Write to: `openspec/changes/issue-${{ issue.number }}/specs/<capability>/spec.md`
</output>

<instruction>
Create one spec file per capability listed in the proposal's Capabilities section, using the exact kebab-case name from the proposal.

Read the existing code to understand current behavior. Specs describe the target behavior for THIS change directly — do not write `## ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED` delta sections, and do not reference any other spec file. Each spec is a self-contained statement of what this change requires.

If a capability introduces new behavior, state the new requirements. If a capability changes existing behavior, state the resulting requirement as it should be after this change (read the code to capture what exists today, then describe the target).
</instruction>

<template>
<!-- Use this as the structure for each spec file. Fill in the requirements. -->
### Requirement: <!-- requirement name -->
<!-- requirement text -->

#### Scenario: <!-- scenario name -->
- **WHEN** <!-- condition -->
- **THEN** <!-- expected outcome -->
</template>

<format>
- Start each spec file directly with requirement blocks (no `## ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED` headers).
- Each requirement: `### Requirement: <name>` followed by description.
- Use SHALL/MUST for normative requirements. Avoid should/may.
- Each scenario: `#### Scenario: <name>` with WHEN/THEN format.
- CRITICAL: Scenarios MUST use exactly 4 hashtags (`####`). Using 3 hashtags or bullets will fail silently.
- Every requirement MUST have at least one scenario.
</format>

<success_criteria>
- Every capability listed in proposal.md has a corresponding spec file.
- Every requirement has at least one scenario.
- Specs are testable and use normative SHALL/MUST language.
- Specs describe target behavior directly, without delta operation headers.
</success_criteria>

</artifact>
